\section{Introduction}

Hybrid linear recurrent architectures---including GDN, RetNet, HGRN2, GLA, and DeltaNet---achieve aggregate benchmark scores within 1--2 points of each other at matched parameter budgets. Yet they can differ on up to 90\% of the individual examples they affect: adding attention to three controlled linear recurrent backbones yields helped and hurt sets with at most 10\% pairwise overlap, approximately twice the independence baseline. This per-example divergence is invisible in aggregate metrics: architectures that score within 1--2 points of each other reach similar accuracy by gaining and losing on different inputs. Across all ten pairwise comparisons among five architectures, overlap in per-example effects remains below 14\% (all $p < .01$), meaning the choice of backbone is associated with which specific inputs a deployed system handles correctly.

This tension arises in the context of a rapid architectural shift toward hybrid recurrent-attention models, which interleave attention layers within linear recurrent backbones. Jamba \citep{lieber2024jamba}, Griffin \citep{de2024griffin}, RWKV-6 \citep{peng2024eagle}, and RecurrentGemma \citep{botev2024recurrentgemma} each combine recurrence with attention using different mixing strategies. These systems now serve as production-grade alternatives to pure Transformers, and the practical question of how much attention to include is typically answered by comparing aggregate benchmark numbers. \citet{wang2025hybrid} provide a controlled model zoo spanning three recurrent architectures, five attention ratios, and two parameter scales, which enables systematic study of how attention allocation affects these hybrid designs.

Prior work on predictive multiplicity \citep{marx2020predictive} and underspecification \citep{damour2022underspecification,mccoy2020berts} documents this risk for seed variation within a single architecture, but whether it extends to architectural variation in hybrid recurrent models remains open. If architectures that score similarly in aggregate behave differently on individual inputs, the backbone choice carries hidden consequences for deployment reliability and ensemble construction.

We present the first systematic per-example analysis of how attention affects cross-architecture agreement in hybrid recurrent models. Using the model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}, we compare three architectures---GatedDeltaNet (gated delta rules), RetNet (multi-scale exponential decay), and HGRN2 (gated state expansion)---at five attention ratios (0--25\%) and two scales (340M, 1.3B). We evaluate all 30 configurations on 10 benchmarks spanning retrieval, comprehension, reasoning, and mixed categories. On four benchmarks (LAMBADA, $N = 5{,}153$; HellaSwag, $N = 10{,}042$; PIQA, $N = 1{,}838$; ARC-Easy, $N = 2{,}376$) we conduct per-example decompositions, classifying each example as helped, hurt, or unchanged when attention is added, and measure cross-architecture overlap via Jaccard similarity. Figure~\ref{fig:decoupling} previews our central result: aggregate variance reduction and per-example overlap follow independent trajectories.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_decoupling_hero.pdf}
\caption{Aggregate variance and per-example overlap follow independent trajectories (helped: correct only with attention; hurt: correct only without). (a) Cross-architecture standard deviation on LAMBADA decreases with attention ratio at 1.3B (solid; Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .170$, non-significant) but partially rebounds at 340M (dashed). (b) Cross-architecture pairwise Jaccard similarity of per-example helped/hurt sets saturates at the first attention level (4\%) and remains flat. Shaded bands are illustrative ($n = 3$).}
\label{fig:decoupling}
\end{figure}

Our analysis yields two findings. First, per-example predictive multiplicity varies systematically across tasks. The ambiguity $\alpha$ \citep{marx2020predictive} ranges from 0.14 (PIQA) through 0.15 (HellaSwag) and 0.21 (ARC-Easy) to 0.30 (LAMBADA), while pairwise Jaccard remains uniformly low ($\leq 0.10$, all $p < .01$). Within each benchmark, $\alpha$ is stable across attention ratios (variation $< 0.03$) but differs $2\times$ across task types (reasoning to retrieval).

Second, per-example overlap is independent of aggregate variance trends. Of 10 benchmarks, only LAMBADA shows a non-significant decreasing variance pattern ($\rho = -1.0$, Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .170$; observed at 1.3B, absent at 340M). Yet even on this benchmark, per-example Jaccard saturates at the first attention level while aggregate variance continues to decrease (\S\ref{ssec:decoupling}).

Our contributions are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Architectural variation---a source of multiplicity not previously characterized at the per-example level---is associated with structured predictive multiplicity ($\alpha = 0.14$--$0.30$) across four benchmarks, with magnitude determined by task type rather than attention dose (\S\ref{ssec:per_example}, Table~\ref{tab:multiplicity}). Validation across five architectures confirms the pattern: all ten pairwise Jaccard values remain $\leq 0.14$ (all $p < .01$; Appendix~\ref{app:extended_arch}). The primary controlled variable is the recurrent backbone \citep[cf.][]{mccoy2020berts, damour2022underspecification}, though single training runs prevent full disentanglement from seed effects (Limitations).
    \item Per-example Jaccard saturates at the first attention level (4\%) across all four benchmarks, while aggregate variance shows a non-significant decreasing pattern on LAMBADA (\S\ref{ssec:decoupling}; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_bench_jaccard}). Minimal attention suffices to establish the full pattern of configuration-specific per-example effects; additional attention narrows aggregate gaps without homogenizing which examples each architecture affects. The aggregate variance pattern depends on GDN inclusion (\S\ref{ssec:robustness}) and does not replicate on other benchmarks.
\end{itemize}

