\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Hybrid Recurrent Architectures.}
The design space for combining attention with recurrence spans layer-level interleaving \citep[Jamba;][]{lieber2024jamba}, local attention pairing \citep[Griffin, RecurrentGemma;][]{de2024griffin,botev2024recurrentgemma}, matrix-valued recurrence \citep[RWKV-6;][]{peng2024eagle}, within-head mixing \citep[Hymba;][]{dong2024hymba}, and unbounded-context hybrid SSMs \citep[Samba;][]{ren2024samba}. H3 \citep{fu2023hungry} and Hyena \citep{poli2023hyena} establish early design points in the hybrid space with gated convolutions and subquadratic operators. Mamba-2 \citep{dao2024mamba2} establishes a duality between structured state spaces and attention; Mamba-3 \citep{lahoti2026mamba3} extends the design further, while \citet{ali2025hidden} reveal implicit attention within Mamba. Comparative evaluations confirm that hybrids outperform pure Transformers and SSMs at scale \citep{waleffe2024empirical}, and that even one attention layer enables content-based routing \citep{basu2026routing}. Recent theoretical work establishes a provable expressiveness hierarchy between hybrid and pure linear attention \citep{ye2026provable}, providing formal grounding for the architectural differences we characterize at the per-example level. \citet{wang2025hybrid} provide the controlled model zoo we use, holding training conditions constant while varying architecture and attention ratio.

\paragraph{Linear Recurrent Models.}
Our three recurrent backbones represent distinct gating strategies within the linear recurrence family: RetNet \citep{sun2023retnet} uses multi-scale exponential decay, HGRN2 \citep{qin2024hgrn2} applies gated state expansion, and GatedDeltaNet \citep{yang2024gateddeltanet} combines gated updates with delta rules. These descend from S4 \citep{gu2022s4} and Mamba \citep{gu2024mamba}, which introduced structured state spaces and input-dependent selection, respectively, and connect to linear attention \citep{katharopoulos2020transformers} through the state-space--attention duality of Mamba-2.

\paragraph{Underspecification and Predictive Multiplicity.}
\citet{damour2022underspecification} formalized underspecification: equivalent aggregate performance can mask per-prediction divergence. NLP instances include seed-dependent BERT disagreement \citep{mccoy2020berts,sellam2022multiberts,dodge2020finetuning} and four-phase convergence in autoregressive LMs \citep{fehlauer2025convergence}. \citet{marx2020predictive} introduced predictive multiplicity as a complementary lens (extending prediction churn; \citealt{milanifard2016launch}), showing that near-optimal models produce conflicting per-example predictions; extensions address probabilistic classification \citep{watson2023predictive}, Rashomon capacity \citep{hsu2022rashomon}, reconciliation \citep{du2025reconciling,haghighat2026resolving}, and cross-model disagreement as a correctness signal \citep{gorbett2026crossmodel}. Surveys by \citet{black2022model} and \citet{ganesh2025systemizing} map the broader landscape; \citet{cooper2024arbitrariness} connect arbitrariness to fairness.

These studies attribute divergence to initialization noise or the near-optimal set within a single architecture. Rashomon-set analyses \citep{fisher2019all,semenova2022existence,jain2025allocation} examine variation among near-optimal models within a single hypothesis class; ensemble diversity work \citep{fort2019deep,kuncheva2003measures,brown2005diversity} shows that diverse architectures explore distinct loss-landscape modes. Weight-space interpolation \citep{wortsman2022model} can reduce multiplicity among models of a single architecture; we instead characterize multiplicity across architectures, where interpolation is not applicable. \citet{jiang2024predictive} extend multiplicity analysis to foundation models in the fine-tuning regime; our work examines the pre-training regime under architectural variation. We show that changing the recurrent backbone---without explicit diversification---is associated with per-example multiplicity that varies systematically by task type. \citet{swayamdipta2020dataset} characterize examples via training dynamics; our decomposition operates at deployment time across architectures. Our cross-benchmark variation ($\alpha = 0.14$--$0.30$) argues against single-artifact explanations \citep{niven2019probing}. The observation that aggregate associations can diverge from individual-level patterns is a well-known statistical phenomenon (ecological fallacy; \citealt{robinson1950ecological}); our decoupling finding (\S\ref{ssec:decoupling}) is a controlled instance within recurrent architectural families.

